WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.600
Speaker 1: All right, let's go next to Mike&nbsp;
from Massachusetts, Mike. You're on the air.&nbsp;

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.760
Speaker 3: Eight on the day, David, I'm doing&nbsp;
well. But here I got two questions if we can,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.680
if we have the time, please first, and&nbsp;
if you've already spoken about this on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:21.760
your show and I missed it, I apologize for the&nbsp;
repetition. What do you make of what's going on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:27.600
in Austria with their nationwide mandate&nbsp;
and that other countries in Europe are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:35.840
starting to inch towards doing the same thing? Do&nbsp;
you think a nationwide vaccine mandate? I mean, do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:42.240
you see that as simply a step too far for Western&nbsp;
democracy? Or is it kind of? Or do you feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:47.680
maybe we reached a point where the extreme&nbsp;
circumstances will require extreme solutions?&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:54.720
Speaker 1: Listen, here. Here's my view of dire&nbsp;
circumstances require extreme solutions. My view&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.520
is as follows I'm trying to think about this&nbsp;
as pragmatically as possible. My opinion on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:06.000
this is heavily biased by the fact that it's&nbsp;
not going to happen in the United States. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.520
when someone says to me, do you think it's too&nbsp;
far or not, might it happen? The United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:16.560
is not going to have a literal vaccine mandate&nbsp;
where the government says you must be vaccinated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:23.120
They're doing stuff through employers O-Shot.&nbsp;
Employers must test or Viagra, but it's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.440
not going to happen. And so because I&nbsp;
see that it's just kind of a nonstarter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.840
It's hard for me to say it would be the&nbsp;
right or the wrong thing, necessarily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:39.840
I mean, I think it is morally defensible. At&nbsp;
the same time, I think there are good arguments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:46.000
Legally speaking, for a while, it can be a work&nbsp;
requirement. It can be a school requirement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.016
It can be a requirement to avail yourself of&nbsp;
certain types of businesses, but just a straight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.016 --> 00:01:55.520
up mandate. I don't know legally whether&nbsp;
it would stand in a completely broad based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:02.960
federal way, although there's people on both&nbsp;
sides of that. I think my issue at this point is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:08.560
what is most likely to most quickly get us out of&nbsp;
the pandemic. And by that, I mean, to an endemic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:15.200
phase, it doesn't seem like it's going to go&nbsp;
away, and it is about convincing the people we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:23.920
can convince to go and get vaccinated. And also,&nbsp;
to some degree, it's about the reality that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:30.240
don't interact with everyone like the fact that&nbsp;
there is a big anti-vax community in Mississippi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.280
is statistically very unlikely to&nbsp;
affect my exposure to the virus. Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.640
I might be on a plane with folks, but for the&nbsp;
most part, it's like what's going on in my kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:43.520
of more immediate community travel spreads, etc.,&nbsp;
etc. But I'm trying to focus more on sort of like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:47.840
what can I do within my immediate community? And&nbsp;
so to go back to your question, do I think it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:53.680
right or wrong with Austria's doing? I don't fully&nbsp;
know everything that's taking place in Austria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.960
but it's not for me to say that they are right or&nbsp;
wrong for doing it. This is what they're doing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:02.320
I don't think it's going to happen in&nbsp;
the United States. So my concern is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:07.360
how can we get beyond the pandemic phase? Given&nbsp;
that there's not going to be a full on vaccine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.240
mandate, that's I'm thinking pragmatically.
Speaker 3: So I agree with you that it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:17.680
extremely unlikely to happen at the federal&nbsp;
level. That would be a very big uphill battle for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:23.600
any administration to take on. But do&nbsp;
you see do you see a certain point where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:28.720
maybe states might try to impose a statewide&nbsp;
vaccine? I really don't. I really don't like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.480
you know, by the Supreme Court in the past.
Speaker 1: Yeah, I know it has. And there's this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.200
very important case from Cambridge, Massachusetts,&nbsp;
and I think it was 1993 that people point to as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:43.600
precedent. I think that Republican governors&nbsp;
would never do it. And Democratic governors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:48.400
realize it would be politically problematic. So&nbsp;
I don't think we're even going to see a mandate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:51.760
at the state level. I could be wrong. If&nbsp;
I'm wrong, I'll eat my socks, you know?&nbsp;

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:57.440
Speaker 3: Fair enough. All right. Switching gears&nbsp;
to a second question, I'll try to keep things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:03.360
moving. Sure. So this is this might be purely&nbsp;
opinion oriented at this point, but so the Supreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:09.200
Court is hearing the Missouri abortion case&nbsp;
today released this hearing arguments on it. Yep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:14.160
and I've been here. I've been trying to do my own&nbsp;
research and look into what certain experts think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:20.320
about what is going to happen and the opinions are&nbsp;
all over the map. You know, some people think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:24.640
this should be so obvious that it's&nbsp;
unconstitutional, that the mere fact that they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:29.440
hearing it is a bad sign. Yes. But then there are,&nbsp;
you know, there are conservatives who are like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:33.280
the Supreme Court always lets us down. It's not.&nbsp;
It's going to be the same case this time. So.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:37.760
And obviously, that's not me saying let&nbsp;
down, but that's the conservatives saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:43.040
that they should get let down. I mean, do you&nbsp;
what's your opinion on how this is all going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:48.320
shake out? I mean, are we looking at the end&nbsp;
of Roe v. Wade or is this going to get shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:52.000
down? Conservatives are going to freak out again.
Speaker 1: I have no idea. I really don't. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.800
it's I've read some of the same stuff you've read,&nbsp;
it sounds like, and I don't even bother making a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:02.240
prediction because it's so unclear. It's sort&nbsp;
of a wacky Supreme Court. It's strange times.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:06.640
I think anything could happen. I do think&nbsp;
that if one of these really strict laws&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:10.960
is upheld by the Supreme Court, it is the&nbsp;
beginning of the end for Roe v. Wade. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:13.600
as far as what is going to happen,&nbsp;
I have no idea we were going to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:15.200
to wait and see.
Speaker 3: OK,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:19.920
fair enough. Thank you for taking my call.
Speaker 1: All right, Mike from Massachusetts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:24.800
Thank you so much for the call. Appreciate&nbsp;
everybody we were able to speak to. That's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:29.760
to do it for today, but we will take calls again&nbsp;
soon. And remember, December 14th at six p.m. will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:36.080
be a wide open viewer town hall where everyone&nbsp;
is welcome to call in more on that upcoming.

